<section class="sample view-tracked" id="ce" aria-labelledby="h-ce">
<header class="sh"><h2 id="h-ce">Journal article copyedit</h2><p class="brief">Two paragraphs from the abstract of a qualitative health study.</p></header>
<div class="switch" role="group" aria-label="Choose view">
<button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-view="tracked">Tracked changes</button>
<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-view="clean">My edit</button>
<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-view="original">Original</button></div>
<div class="work"><div class="text"><p>Method: Focus group interviews were used to explore how young women (aged 15–19) experience <ins class="ins">f</ins><del class="del">F</del>at <ins class="ins">t</ins><del class="del">T</del>alk whilst using social media<del class="del">,</del> and <ins class="ins">its </ins>possible effect on body image constructs.<del class="del"> Using purposive convenience sampling,</del> <del class="del">y</del><ins class="ins">Y</ins>oung women who regularly used social media<ins class="ins"> </ins>and were living in an inner<ins class="ins">-</ins><del class="del"> </del>city <ins class="ins">area </ins>of England were recruited<ins class="ins"> using purposive convenience sampling</ins>. Thematic analysis was used<ins class="ins">,</ins> <del class="del">for analysis </del>and six themes were identified, both <ins class="ins">a </ins><del class="del">a-</del>priori and inductively, to explore the interplay between <span class="q">them</span><sup class="ref" id="refce-1"><a href="#notece-1" aria-label="Query 1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Findings: <span class="q">Over 35 women </span><sup class="ref" id="refce-2"><a href="#notece-2" aria-label="Query 2">2</a></sup>were <del class="del">successfully </del>recruited into the study, <del class="del">with </del>18 of <ins class="ins">whom </ins><del class="del">these finally </del>participat<ins class="ins">ed</ins><del class="del">ing</del> in focus group interviews. Among<del class="del">st</del> <ins class="ins">the </ins>participants, social media use was linked to increased self-evaluation of the body, engagement in <ins class="ins">social </ins><del class="del">social comparative </del><ins class="ins">comparison </ins>behaviour<del class="del">,</del> and negative self-talk about the body. Although<ins class="ins"> f</ins><del class="del"> F</del>at <ins class="ins">t</ins><del class="del">T</del>alk was reportedly common and widespread, it was <ins class="ins">considered </ins>unacceptable <del class="del">to conduct </del>in the online space. However<ins class="ins">,</ins> body<ins class="ins"> </ins><del class="del">-</del>talk<ins class="ins"> about aspects</ins><del class="del">,</del> other than size or shape<del class="del">,</del> <del class="del">is </del><ins class="ins">was </ins>permissible.</p></div>
<aside class="notes" aria-label="Queries to the author"><h3>Queries</h3><ol><li id="notece-1"><span class="n">1</span><p>What is “them” referring to?</p></li>
<li id="notece-2"><span class="n">2</span><p>Please give the exact number</p></li></ol></aside></div></section>
<section class="sample view-tracked" id="pt" aria-labelledby="h-pt">
<header class="sh"><h2 id="h-pt">Oncology abstract edit</h2><p class="brief">Two paragraphs from a breast cancer case-control study by an author writing in English as a second language. The brief asked me to check for sentence fragments, incoherent ideas, and errors in language and technical terminology.</p></header>
<div class="switch" role="group" aria-label="Choose view">
<button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-view="tracked">Tracked changes</button>
<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-view="clean">My edit</button>
<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-view="original">Original</button></div>
<div class="work"><div class="text"><p>Methods: This <ins class="ins">was</ins><del class="del">is</del> a retrospective<ins class="ins">,</ins> hospital<ins class="ins">-</ins><del class="del"> </del>base<ins class="ins">d</ins> case<ins class="ins">-</ins><del class="del"> </del>control <span class="q">study of </span><del class="del q">all </del><span class="q">Saudi </span><del class="del q">female </del><ins class="ins q">women </ins><span class="q">diagnosed with breast cancer from 2010 to 2011</span><ins class="ins q">.</ins><del class="del q">,</del><span class="q"> </span><ins class="ins q">There were </ins><span class="q">225 </span><del class="del q">breast cancer patients </del><sup class="ref" id="refpt-1"><a href="#notept-1" aria-label="Query 1">1</a></sup><ins class="ins">patients </ins>(n=<del class="del"> </del>225) and 450 age-matched control<ins class="ins">s</ins>. The <del class="del">statistical </del>analyses <del class="del">were using </del><ins class="ins">comprised </ins>descriptive statistics, <ins class="ins q">S</ins><del class="del q">S</del><span class="q">tudent</span><ins class="ins q">’</ins><span class="q">s </span><del class="del q">Sample </del><span class="q">t</span><ins class="ins q">-</ins><del class="del q"> </del><span class="q">test</span><ins class="ins q">s</ins><span class="q">, Man</span><del class="del q">-</del><ins class="ins q">n-</ins><span class="q">Whit</span><del class="del q">t</del><span class="q">ney </span><sup class="ref" id="refpt-2"><a href="#notept-2" aria-label="Query 2">2</a></sup>U test<ins class="ins">s</ins>, un<ins class="ins">i</ins>variate odds ratio (OR) <del class="del">analysis</del><ins class="ins">estimation</ins>, <ins class="ins">and both </ins><del class="del q">unmatched </del><ins class="ins q">unconditional </ins><del class="del q">ordinary logistic regression mode </del><span class="q">and </span><del class="del q">matched </del><span class="q">conditional logistic regression model</span><ins class="ins q">s,</ins><span class="q"> </span><sup class="ref" id="refpt-3"><a href="#notept-3" aria-label="Query 3">3</a></sup><ins class="ins">performed </ins><del class="del">with the </del><ins class="ins">in </ins>SPSS<ins class="ins"> version</ins><del class="del">,</del> 20. </p>
<p>Results: In <del class="del">matched </del><ins class="ins">the </ins>conditional logistic regression model, <del class="del">the early </del>marriage or <del class="del">early </del>sex<ins class="ins">ual intercourse </ins><ins class="ins q">before the age of 18 years</ins><span class="q"> </span><sup class="ref" id="refpt-4"><a href="#notept-4" aria-label="Query 4">4</a></sup><del class="del">i.e., females &lt;18 years of age </del>(<ins class="ins">OR</ins><del class="del">or</del>=15.6, 95% CI<del class="del">,</del> 5.92<ins class="ins">-</ins><del class="del"> to </del>41.2) <del class="del">were </del><ins class="ins">showed </ins>the strongest <ins class="ins">association with </ins><del class="del">risk factors for </del>breast cancer among women<del class="del"> in the Eastern region of Saudi Arabia</del>. <span class="q">Obesity</span><ins class="ins q">,</ins><span class="q"> </span><del class="del q">with </del><ins class="ins q">i.e., </ins><del class="del q">high body mass index (</del><span class="q">BMI</span><del class="del q">) </del><sup class="ref" id="refpt-5"><a href="#notept-5" aria-label="Query 5">5</a></sup><span class="q">≥30</span><ins class="ins q"> (</ins><del class="del q"> (</del><span class="q">OR=12.3, 95</span><del class="del q"> </del><span class="q">% CI</span><del class="del q">,</del><span class="q"> 4.88</span><ins class="ins q">-</ins><del class="del q"> to </del><span class="q">31.2)</span><ins class="ins q">,</ins><del class="del q">,</del><span class="q"> </span><span class="q">a</span><sup class="ref" id="refpt-6"><a href="#notept-6" aria-label="Query 6">6</a></sup><span class="q">nd </span><del class="del q">late </del><span class="q">menopause </span><ins class="ins q">at 50 years or later </ins><del class="del q">among women ≥ 50 years of old </del><span class="q">(</span><ins class="ins q">OR</ins><del class="del q">or</del><span class="q">=7.0, 95% CI</span><del class="del q">,</del><span class="q"> 1.30</span><ins class="ins q">-</ins><del class="del q"> to </del><span class="q">37.9)</span><ins class="ins q"> were also associated with increased risk</ins><span class="q">. </span><sup class="ref" id="refpt-7"><a href="#notept-7" aria-label="Query 7">7</a></sup>The <ins class="ins q">most significant </ins><del class="del q">highest significance </del><span class="q">protective factor</span><ins class="ins q">s </ins><sup class="ref" id="refpt-8"><a href="#notept-8" aria-label="Query 8">8</a></sup><ins class="ins">observed </ins><del class="del"> for female breast cancer </del>were <ins class="ins">women who were </ins>breastfeeding (OR=0.1, 95% CI<del class="del">,</del> 0.03<ins class="ins">-</ins><del class="del"> to </del>0.22),<ins class="ins"> had a</ins> <span class="q">higher education</span><sup class="ref" id="refpt-9"><a href="#notept-9" aria-label="Query 9">9</a></sup> (OR=0.05, 95% CI<del class="del">,</del> 0.01<ins class="ins">-</ins><del class="del"> to </del>0.28), <ins class="ins">were </ins><del class="del q">and marital status that includes </del><span class="q">divorced </span><del class="del q">women </del><span class="q">(</span><ins class="ins q">OR</ins><del class="del q">or</del><span class="q">=0.05, 95% CI</span><del class="del q">,</del><span class="q"> 0.01</span><ins class="ins q">-</ins><del class="del q"> to </del><span class="q">0.32), </span><del class="del q">and </del><ins class="ins q">or were </ins><span class="q">widowed </span><sup class="ref" id="refpt-10"><a href="#notept-10" aria-label="Query 10">10</a></sup>(<ins class="ins">OR</ins><del class="del">or</del>=0.17, 95% CI, 0.04<ins class="ins">-</ins><del class="del"> to </del>0.71).</p></div>
<aside class="notes" aria-label="Queries to the author"><h3>Queries</h3><ol><li id="notept-1"><span class="n">1</span><p>Since the number of women studied were 225, saying “all” is in contrast to that statement. 225 is a subset.</p></li>
<li id="notept-2"><span class="n">2</span><p>Important to get the names of the tools right and keep them consistent</p></li>
<li id="notept-3"><span class="n">3</span><p>The widely accepted terms are “unconditional and conditional logistics regression models”</p></li>
<li id="notept-4"><span class="n">4</span><p>“&lt;18 years of age” describes the woman’s current age, which is not what is meant here.</p></li>
<li id="notept-5"><span class="n">5</span><p>Obesity is defined as such so stating it twice is repetition.</p></li>
<li id="notept-6"><span class="n">6</span><p>Is there a particular reason this portion is in a formula box?</p></li>
<li id="notept-7"><span class="n">7</span><p>This sentence has no verb.</p></li>
<li id="notept-8"><span class="n">8</span><p>Consider reframing as “associated with lower risks” as a defensible positioning.</p></li>
<li id="notept-9"><span class="n">9</span><p>To what level? High school, bachelor’s, master’s?</p></li>
<li id="notept-10"><span class="n">10</span><p>What is the biological pathway to these? Is the presence of a partner somehow related to the presence of breast cancer? This might need some deeper explanation rather than just being stated as a metric.</p></li></ol></aside></div></section>
<section class="sample view-tracked" id="wv" aria-labelledby="h-wv">
<header class="sh"><h2 id="h-wv">Humanities paper language edit</h2><p class="brief">A section of a paper on masculinity in country music. The brief asked for a substantive language edit with comments explaining revisions.</p></header>
<div class="switch" role="group" aria-label="Choose view">
<button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-view="tracked">Tracked changes</button>
<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-view="clean">My edit</button>
<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-view="original">Original</button></div>
<div class="work"><div class="text"><p>Country (Music) <del class="del">Maleness </del><ins class="ins">Masculinity </ins>is a Conflicted Identity</p>
<p><span class="q">Country music </span><del class="del q">at </del><ins class="ins q">is </ins><span class="q">some</span><ins class="ins q">times</ins><span class="q"> </span><del class="del q">points is </del><span class="q">seen as </span><span class="q">opposing a norm</span><sup class="ref" id="refwv-1"><a href="#notewv-1" aria-label="Query 1">1</a></sup><span class="q">, valuing traditionalism and conservati</span><ins class="ins q">ve </ins><del class="del q">onal </del><span class="q">values over the </span><del class="del q">mainstream’s </del><span class="q">diverse but establishment views</span><ins class="ins q"> </ins><sup class="ref" id="refwv-2"><a href="#notewv-2" aria-label="Query 2">2</a></sup><sup class="ref" id="refwv-3"><a href="#notewv-3" aria-label="Query 3">3</a></sup><ins class="ins q">of the mainstream</ins><del class="del q"> </del><span class="q">, </span><del class="del q">with </del><ins class="ins q">and having </ins><span class="q">a regressive or even reactionary notion of masculinity </span><sup class="ref" id="refwv-4"><a href="#notewv-4" aria-label="Query 4">4</a></sup>(Tremmel 1995; Knox 2000; Bernstein 2016). A claim that runs counter to this view <del class="del">held </del><ins class="ins">holds </ins>that rural conservative masculinity is still seen as the <del class="del">most </del>real masculin<ins class="ins">ity</ins><del class="del">e</del> <del class="del">ideal </del>in the <del class="del">u</del><ins class="ins">U</ins>nited <del class="del">s</del><ins class="ins">S</ins>tates (O’Reilly. 2016). This interplay<del class="del">ing </del> between urban and country reflects <ins class="ins">Connell’s </ins><del class="del">Connell </del>(1995) assertion that the dominant or hegemonic<del class="del">,</del> form of masculinity <ins class="ins">in a </ins><del class="del">at a </del>certain place <del class="del">or </del><ins class="ins">and </ins>time <span class="q">is </span><ins class="ins q">a contested one</ins><del class="del q">a given that it will be contested</del><sup class="ref" id="refwv-5"><a href="#notewv-5" aria-label="Query 5">5</a></sup>, w<ins class="ins">here</ins><del class="del">ith</del> the masculine ideals that are glorified in the country genre exist in contrast not only to the femininity ideals present within the genre but <ins class="ins">also </ins>with the masculine images present in rock, rap and other genres<del class="del"> as well that have masculine images</del>. <ins class="ins">Effectively, </ins><del class="del"> In effect, </del>while country music maleness can be marginal or oppositional outside of the country music audience, it can at the same time be the hegemonic ideal within that audience</p></div>
<aside class="notes" aria-label="Queries to the author"><h3>Queries</h3><ol><li id="notewv-1"><span class="n">1</span><p>What kind of norm does it oppose if it values traditionalism and conservatism?</p></li>
<li id="notewv-2"><span class="n">2</span><p>This needs some clarification. What exactly is meant by “establishment views”?</p></li>
<li id="notewv-3"><span class="n">3</span><p>Is this meant to be “established” views?</p></li>
<li id="notewv-4"><span class="n">4</span><p>Sentence is run-on and holds too much information</p></li>
<li id="notewv-5"><span class="n">5</span><p>I think you mean to say “it is a contested form”</p></li></ol></aside></div></section>
<section class="sample view-tracked" id="es" aria-labelledby="h-es">
<header class="sh"><h2 id="h-es">Personal essay edit</h2><p class="brief">An undergraduate business school application essay on what makes the applicant unique and what value they would add.</p></header>
<div class="switch" role="group" aria-label="Choose view">
<button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-view="tracked">Tracked changes</button>
<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-view="clean">My edit</button>
<button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-view="original">Original</button></div>
